To add metadata to path parameters and include extra information in the OpenAPI schema for both path parameters and request body fields in FastAPI, you can use several techniques:

### 1. Adding Metadata to Path Parameters

You can use `Path()` from FastAPI to declare path parameters with metadata. This allows you to specify descriptions, titles, and constraints for the parameters.

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Path

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/items/{item_id}")
async def read_item(
    item_id: int = Path(..., title="The ID of the item to retrieve", ge=0)
):
    return {"item_id": item_id}
```

In this example, `item_id` has a title and a constraint that ensures it is greater than or equal to zero. This metadata will be reflected in the OpenAPI schema and in the generated documentation. [source_url](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/path-params/) [source_url](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/reference/parameters/)

### 2. Including Extra Information in OpenAPI Schema

For adding extra information to both path parameters and request bodies in your OpenAPI schema, you can use the `openapi_extra` parameter when defining a path operation. This parameter accepts a dictionary that would be merged into the automatically generated schema.

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Request
from fastapi.openapi.models import RequestBody

app = FastAPI()

@app.post(
    "/items/",
    openapi_extra={
        "requestBody": {
            "content": {
                "application/json": {
                    "schema": {
                        "type": "object",
                        "properties": {
                            "name": {"type": "string"},
                            "price": {"type": "number"}
                        },
                        "required": ["name", "price"]
                    }
                }
            },
            "required": True,
        },
    },
)
async def create_item(request: Request):
    data = await request.json()
    return {"item": data}
```

In this case, the `openapi_extra` dictionary provides a custom schema for the request body, which will appear in the OpenAPI schema generated for this endpoint. [source_url](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/advanced/path-operation-advanced-configuration/) [source_url](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/body/)

### 3. Using Body for Extra Request Information

For the body parameters, you can use the `Body()` function to define singular values treated as part of the body rather than as query parameters:

```python
from fastapi import Body

@app.put("/items/{item_id}")
async def update_item(
    item_id: int,
    name: str = Body(..., title="The name of the item"),
    price: float = Body(..., gt=0, title="The price of the item")
):
    return {"item_id": item_id, "name": name, "price": price}
```

Here, `name` and `price` include titles and constraints, creating clear documentation. [source_url](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/body/)

By using these techniques, you can effectively enhance the OpenAPI documentation generated by FastAPI, providing users with better insights into the API parameters and their constraints.

